.TH VPRINTF 3S "June 23, 1989"
.ds ]W ANSI C
.SH NAME
vfprintf, vprintf, vsprintf \-
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdarg.h>
.br
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
\fBint vfprintf(FILE *stream, const char *format, va_list arg);\fR
.PP
\fBint vprintf(const char *format, va_list arg);\fR
.PP
\fBint vsprintf(char *s, const char *format, va_list arg);\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Vfprintf
is equivalent to
.BR fprintf ,
.B vprintf
is equivalent to
.BR printf ,
and
.B vsprintf
is equivalent to
.BR sprintf ,
with the variable argument list replaced in each case by
.BR arg ,
which has been initialized by the
.B va_start
macro
(and possibly by subsequent
.B va_arg
calls).
The
.B vfprintf
function does not invoke the
.B va_end
macro.
.SH "RETURN VALUE
.BR Vfprintf ,
.BR vprintf ,
and
.B vsprintf
return the number of characters transmitted,
or a negative value if an output error occurred.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
fprintf(3), stdarg(3)

